From f89b089b069dc027aea103a689ab06d2d051ecb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 00:55:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 32/40] lib/promscrape: keep delta acknowledgements across
 config changes, push delta rounds whole

Two ways a target exposing increments could lose a round or have one counted
twice.

A scrapeWork is replaced whenever its scrape config or target labels change,
as editing sample_limit does, and the acknowledgement state lived on it: the new
one started from AckNew, which the target answers by committing the round the
old one had not processed, with every round rolled into it. The state now lives
per scrape URL, in a registry the replacing scrapeWork picks up; states unused
for an hour are dropped by a janitor.

Stream parsing pushes each block as it is parsed. A response failed partway --
sample_limit or label_limit exceeded in a later block, or compressed data
damaged past the first blocks -- fails the scrape after some blocks were
pushed; the round is not acknowledged, the target sends it again with the next
round, and the blocks pushed already count twice. The whole response is in
memory before parsing starts, so a delta round is now read through first,
pushing nothing, and only pushed if all of it can be. Without limits only the
decompression can fail, and only that is done: about 4 GB/s.

Tests: a replaced scrapeWork goes on acknowledging what the old one processed;
a round failing on sample_limit (streamed and with the body kept), and one with
damaged zstd, push nothing of 200000 samples where 199864, 197816 and 156621
were pushed before; a round that fits goes out whole.
